COB - AWS Infrastructure Platform
COB is an Infrastructure as Code project developed for Beejan Technologies Platform Engineering using Terraform and AWS.
The purpose of COB is to provide reusable Terraform modules that make it easier for application teams to provision AWS infrastructure without building everything from scratch.
The platform focuses on reusable infrastructure, secure defaults, consistent naming and tagging, network isolation, and least-privilege access.

Technologies
The project currently uses:
Terraform
AWS VPC
Amazon S3
AWS IAM
Amazon ECS
AWS Fargate
Amazon CloudWatch
Amazon RDS PostgreSQL

The current development environment is deployed in the us-east-1 AWS region.
Network
The network module provides the networking foundation for the COB environment.

It creates:
A VPC, Two public subnets, Two private subnets, Internet Gateway, NAT Gateway, Public and private route tables,Security groups

The VPC uses the 10.20.0.0/16 CIDR range and resources are distributed across two Availability Zones.
Application workloads such as ECS and RDS use the private subnets. The NAT Gateway allows resources in those private subnets to make outbound connections without giving them public IP addresses.

Object Storage
The storage module provides secure object storage using Amazon S3.

The module includes:
Server-side encryption using AES-256, S3 versioning, Public access blocking, Lifecycle rules,Standard project and environment tags
These settings provide a more secure and operationally useful S3 bucket than creating a basic bucket without additional controls.

IAM

The IAM module provides reusable permissions for workloads running through COB.
A workload IAM role is created for the application running in ECS. The role currently provides access only to the S3 bucket created by the storage module.

The permitted actions are:
s3:ListBucket
s3:GetObject
s3:PutObject
The policy is restricted to the required S3 bucket rather than providing access to every bucket in the AWS account.
This follows the AWS principle of least privilege.
ECS and Fargate
The compute ECS module provides container-based compute using Amazon ECS and AWS Fargate.

The module creates:
ECS cluster, ECS task definition, ECS service, ECS security group, ECS task execution role
CloudWatch log group
The current development workload runs an Nginx container using one Fargate task.

The ECS tasks are placed inside the private subnets and are not assigned public IP addresses.
The application also receives the workload IAM role created by the IAM module, allowing applications running through ECS to securely access permitted AWS services such as S3.
Container logs are sent to Amazon CloudWatch for monitoring and troubleshooting.

Database
The database module provides a relational database using Amazon RDS PostgreSQL.

The development database is configured with:
PostgreSQL, 20 GB GP3 storage, Storage encryption, Automated backups, Private subnet placement, Dedicated database security group, Public access disabled
The database security group only allows PostgreSQL traffic on port 5432 from the ECS security group.
This means the database is not directly exposed to the internet and only the application workload is permitted to connect to it.

The development environment uses a smaller and simpler RDS configuration to reduce unnecessary cost. Production settings can later use stronger availability, backup, and deletion-protection settings.

Security
Security is included as part of the COB modules rather than being left entirely to individual application teams.
Current security practices include:
Private subnets for application workloads, Private RDS database, S3 public access blocking, S3 encryption at rest, RDS encryption at rest, Least-privilege IAM permissions
Dedicated ECS and database security groups , Standard naming and tagging
Sensitive Terraform files excluded from Git

Database credentials are currently provided through Terraform variables and are not hardcoded directly into the modules.
Files containing credentials, Terraform state, or other sensitive information should never be committed to GitHub.
Reusable Module Design
COB separates reusable infrastructure modules from environment-specific configuration.
The development environment combines the different modules and passes outputs from one module into another.
For example, the ECS module receives the private subnet IDs created by the network module.
The IAM module receives the S3 bucket ARN created by the storage module.
The database module receives the ECS security group ID so that only ECS workloads can connect to PostgreSQL.
This allows the modules to work together while still remaining reusable.

